If $a = p_1^(a_1) p_2^(a_2) ... p_k^(a_k)$ and $b = p_1^(b_1) p_2^(b_2) ... p_k^(b_k)$ with $a_i, b_i >= 0$ for all $1 <= i <= k$, then
$ gcd(a, b) = p_1^(min(a_1, b_1)) p_2^(min(a_2, b_2)) ... p_k^(min(a_k, b_k)). $
Indeed, by uniqueness of prime factorisation the common factors of $a$ and $b$ are exactly the numbers $p_1^(c_1) p_2^(c_2) ... p_k^(c_k)$ with $0 <= c_i <= min(a_i, b_i)$. For example,
$ gcd(2^3 dot 3^7 dot 5 dot 11^3, 2^4 dot 3^2 dot 11 dot 13) = 2^3 dot 3^2 dot 11. $
